---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CaptureForm from '../components/CaptureForm.jsx';

/* The roadmap. The courses are NOT written here: they are read live from
   /api/roadmap, which reads D1, because a hand-typed copy of something the
   database already knows is a second source of truth waiting to disagree.

   Registration counts stay internal (spec section 2.2). /api/roadmap does not
   even return the count, so no version of this page can leak three zeros. */
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Roadmap: the courses planned, and how the order gets decided"
  description="Three lab-manual courses are planned. Registrations route the build order, not the price. Tell us which one you want first and you hear from us the day it ships."
>
<div class="capp">

  <section class="capp-head">
    <div class="i-shell">
      <p class="i-eyebrow rv">Roadmap</p>
      <h1 class="rv" style="--d:70ms">Three courses planned. You pick the order.</h1>
      <p class="capp-lede rv" style="--d:140ms">
        One module gets written per quarter, so the scarce resource here is time rather than
        ambition. Registrations decide which one is written first. They do not change what anything
        costs, and there is nothing to buy on this page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="i-shell capp-grid">
    <div class="capp-body">

      <section class="capp-sec rv">
        <h2>Why this page exists instead of a pre-sale</h2>
        <p>
          The obvious move is to price each course by demand. We are not doing that. There is no
          demand signal yet, and a per-course price is three guesses published as three numbers to
          defend. Worse, a separately priced course is a one-time purchase: someone takes the one
          module they want, pays once, and never comes back. The membership stays flat and
          all-access, and the growing library is the reason to renew.
        </p>
        <p>
          What demand should route is build order. That is a real decision with a real cost, and it
          is the one your registration actually moves.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="capp-sec rv">
        <h2>What a registration does, exactly</h2>
        <ol class="capp-ledger">
          <li><b>01</b><span><strong>It ranks the build order</strong>The course with the strongest interest is the one written in the next quarterly burst.</span></li>
          <li><b>02</b><span><strong>It buys you one email</strong>Sent when that course ships. Not a drip sequence, not a launch countdown.</span></li>
          <li><b>03</b><span><strong>It can retire a course</strong>If a course draws nothing in six months it does not get built. Learned for free, instead of after thirty lessons.</span></li>
          <li><b>04</b><span><strong>It stores nothing about you here</strong>Your address goes to our newsletter provider. What lands in our database is one number going up by one.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="capp-sec rv">
        <h2>Why there are no counts on this page</h2>
        <p>
          Because a public number is either persuasive or discouraging, and at this stage ours would
          be the second one. Printing it would be asking you to judge the courses by how few people
          have found the page yet. The counts are read internally and they route the build order all
          the same.
        </p>
        <p>
          When they are worth showing they will be read from the database and rendered, never typed
          into the page by hand. The same rule already governs every price on this site.
        </p>
      </section>

      <a class="capp-onward rv" href="/kit/">Get the free kit while you wait</a>
    </div>

    <aside class="capp-aside rv" aria-label="Register interest in a course">
      <CaptureForm variant="roadmap" client:load />
    </aside>
  </div>

</div>
</BaseLayout>
